    <Artboard width="520" height="380" styleId="0:316" x="1708" name="Read Me" id="0:318">
        <LayoutComponentStyle name="Artboard Style" id="0:316"/>
        <Text x="16" y="16" name="Animator Notes" guide="true" id="0:1630">
            <TextStylePaint fontSize="13" fontAssetId="0:54" name="Style" id="0:317">
                <Fill name="Fill" id="0:1631">
                    <SolidColor colorValue="FF170D30" name="Color" id="0:1632"/>
                </Fill>
            </TextStylePaint>
            <TextValueRun styleId="0:317" text="Animator notes (a guide: not exported)
(code) is moved by the script every frame: a key on it is overwritten.
(animate) is yours to key, on top of what the code does:
  Body Pose moves the body, each Foot .. Pose moves that foot
  (both in the spider&apos;s own space: +x is forward),
  Knobs feed values to the script (Tuck: legs drawn in, 0 to 1).
Legs follow their Foot Pose by IK: to pose a leg by hand, key its
  Reach constraint&apos;s strength down first.
The script only decides when: blinkLeft, blinkRight, squint, asleep,
  exertion. The state machine layers play the timelines for them.
Agent modes: the agent enum (Idle, Thinking, Working, Needs You,
  Done) picks a state in the Agent layer, one timeline per mode.
  Rework any Agent timeline freely. A new mode: add an enum value,
  a timeline and a state; keys 0-5 in Desktop set it in the CLI.
Grooming plays the Groom timeline (front Foot Poses), a press plays
  Puff on the Dust motes: both yours to rework.
Silk (the dragline, webs, the hanging thread) is drawn by the Brain
  script, not keyable: its look is the top of spider.luau." name="Run" id="0:1633"/>
        </Text>
    </Artboard>
